secubox-users (1.5.2-1~bookworm1) bookworm; urgency=high

  * SECURITY/DATA-LOSS: the v1->v2 migration destroyed credentials. On gk2 the
    1.4.2 -> 1.5.1 upgrade wiped the argon2 hashes of all three accounts and
    the admin TOTP; the Companion then refused every login with "Aucun mot de
    passe local".
    Cause: `_is_v2()` requires a `version` key, and users.json can lose it to
    any writer that serialises without one. The store then took the v1
    conversion path, which rebuilt each account from a blank template and
    copied only email/role/created — nulling password_hash and forcing
    must_change_password on accounts that were already migrated.
    An array entry is already v2, so it is now overlaid WHOLESALE, secrets
    included. Legacy flat blocks still lose their hash on purpose: those are
    unsalted SHA-256 from auth.toml and cannot become argon2id.
  * The pre-migration backup is now TIMESTAMPED
    (users.json.pre-migrate.<ns>). The old fixed `.v1.bak` name meant a second
    migration overwrote the only good copy with the already-damaged file — on
    gk2 that copy was the one thing that made recovery possible.
  * postinst no longer swallows a migration failure with `|| echo WARN`: a
    step that rewrites the credential store must fail the install, loudly,
    while the snapshot is still fresh. (ref #945)

 -- Gerald KERMA <devel@cybermind.fr>  Sat, 01 Aug 2026 09:30:00 +0200
secubox-users (1.5.1-1~bookworm1) bookworm; urgency=high

  * SECURITY: the per-session Revoke button never revoked anything. It shelled
    out to `curl -X DELETE .../session/{id}` on auth.sock — a route
    secubox-auth has never exposed (its verb is
    `POST /revoke_session?session_id=...`), so every call 404'd. Success was
    then read off curl's exit status, and `curl -s` exits 0 on a 404, so the
    endpoint always answered success:true and the UI reported
    "Session revoked" while the session stayed live.
    Now calls the real route over httpx on the unix socket, forwards the
    CALLER's credentials (secubox-auth requires a JWT and audits `by: <sub>`,
    and #942 allows exactly one writer on the store), and reports the real
    HTTP status.
  * SECURITY: `Revoke All` wrote an OBJECT into sessions.json
    ({"sessions": [], "revoked_at": ...}). The store's only valid shape is a
    list: iterating a dict yields its keys, so secubox-auth's validator raised
    AttributeError on every authenticated request. The panic button broke the
    auth module. Now writes an empty list. (ref #944)

 -- Gerald KERMA <devel@cybermind.fr>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 20:15:00 +0200

